    "result_text": "Full suite green: **283 tests, 0 failures**.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the per-city owner cap: creating an owner is rejected with **400** when the owner's city already contains 8 owners.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerCityCapacityException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` signalling the city has reached its owner limit, mirroring the existing `OwnerRegistrationLimitException`.\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 added a check (only for new owners) that throws `OwnerCityCapacityException` when the city is at capacity. The new `isCityCapacityReached` helper counts existing owners whose city matches case-insensitively against `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 8`; a null city is never capped.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` mapping `OwnerCityCapacityException` \u2192 **400 Bad Request**, consistent with the existing daily-limit handler.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests`** \u2014 the pre-existing daily-cap tests seeded 19\u201320 owners all in \"Madison\", which would now trip the new per-city cap and fail. I gave each seeded owner its own city so those tests continue to exercise the *daily* cap in isolation (their intent), unaffected by the new *per-city* cap. The final POST assertions are unchanged and still pass for the right reason.\n\nThe new `Cp14Tests` acceptance test (8 owners in a city, 9th rejected with 400) passes.",
